              "text": "Typical linebacker numbers depend heavily on whether you are looking at the **traditional history of the game**, the **modern NFL rules**, or **college football**. \n\nThe jersey numbers available to linebackers have changed dramatically in recent years. The breakdown of typical linebacker numbers across these eras and levels of play is detailed below.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Modern NFL Rules (Current)\nUnder current NFL rules (updated in 2021 and 2023), linebackers have immense flexibility and are legally allowed to wear:\n* **0\u201359**\n* **90\u201399**\n\nBecause of this rule change, you will see a massive mix of traditional \"heavy\" numbers and modern, sleek single-digit numbers on Sundays.\n\n---\n\n### 2. The Traditional \"Classic\" Look (The 50s & 90s)\nBefore the NFL relaxed its rules in 2021, linebackers were strictly limited to numbers **40\u201359** and **90\u201399**. Historically, the position was defined by two specific groups:\n\n#### **The 50s (The Standard LB)**\nFor decades, the 50s were the quintessential linebacker numbers. It is the number range most fans immediately associate with hard-hitting inside or middle (\"Mike\") linebackers. \n* **51:** Dick Butkus\n* **52:** Ray Lewis, Patrick Willis\n* **54:** Brian Urlacher, Bobby Wagner, Zach Thomas, Fred Warner\n* **55:** Junior Seau, Derrick Brooks, Mike Singletary\n* **56:** Lawrence Taylor\n\n#### **The 90s (The Edge Rusher / Outside LB)**\nOutside linebackers\u2014especially those who play in 3-4 defensive schemes and act as primary pass rushers\u2014very frequently wear numbers in the 90s. They share this range with defensive linemen.\n* **90:** T.J. Watt\n* **94:** DeMarcus Ware\n* **97:** Joey Bosa (during his time as an outside linebacker/EDGE)\n\n#### **The 40s (The Hybrid LB)**\nThough less common than the 50s, the 40s were historically used for coverage-first linebackers or safety-linebacker hybrids.\n* **48 / 49:** Often used by specialty or hybrid players (e.g., Shaquem Griffin wore 49).\n\n---\n\n### 3. The Modern NFL Trend: Single Digits & Low Numbers\nIn 2021, the NFL allowed linebackers to wear single digits (1\u20139). In 2023, they added the number **0**. This has become incredibly popular for fast, sideline-to-sideline modern linebackers who want to look lighter and quicker.\n\n* **0 (\"Agent Zero\"):** Worn by elite linebackers like Roquan Smith (Ravens) and former Pro-Bowler Brian Burns (Giants).\n* **Single Digits (1\u20139):** For example, Patrick Queen changed his number from 48 to **6** to capture a sleeker look.\n* **The Teens (10\u201319):** Micah Parsons famously wears **11** for the Dallas Cowboys.\n\n---\n\n### 4. College Football (NCAA) Rules\nIn college football, linebackers have almost unlimited freedom. They can wear **any number from 0 to 99**, with the only restriction being that they cannot wear numbers reserved for offensive linemen (**50\u201379**) because those signify ineligible receivers. \n\nBecause of this, college linebackers have worn single digits (like **1, 3, 4, 5, 7, 9, or 11**) for decades. In college, a single-digit number is often a badge of honor awarded to the team's best defensive playmaker.",
